To create a stateless widget in Flutter, you can use the `StatelessWidget` class. This class is a subclass of `Widget`, and it is used to create widgets that do not have their own state management.

To create a stateless widget, you can simply use the `StatelessWidget` class and define the child widgets that you want to appear in the stateless widget. For example:
```
class MyStatelessWidget extends StatelessWidget {
  @override
  Widget build(BuildContext context) {
    return Column(
      children: [
        Text('Hello, world!'),
        ElevatedButton(
          onPressed: () {
            // Handle button press
          },
          child: Text('Press me!'),
        ),
      ],
    );
  }
}
```
In the example above, `MyStatelessWidget` is a stateless widget that displays a `Column` with a `Text` widget and an `ElevatedButton` widget. When the button is pressed, the `onPressed` callback is triggered, but the `MyStatelessWidget` does not maintain its own state.

You can also use the `Scaffold` widget to wrap your stateless widget and provide a layout for the child widgets. For example:
```
class MyStatelessWidget extends StatelessWidget {
  @override
  Widget build(BuildContext context) {
    return Scaffold(
      appBar: AppBar(
        title: Text('My app'),
      ),
      body: Column(
        children: [
          Text('Hello, world!'),
          ElevatedButton(
            onPressed: () {
              // Handle button press
            },
            child: Text('Press me!'),
          ),
        ],
      ),
      floatingActionButton: FloatingActionButton(
        onPressed: () {
          // Handle button press
        },
        child: Text('Floating action button'),
      ),
    );
  }
}
```
In the example above, `MyStatelessWidget` is a stateless widget that displays a `Scaffold` with an `AppBar`, a `Column` with a `Text` widget and an `ElevatedButton`, and a `FloatingActionButton`.
